## 6. Accessor Methods

## 6.1 What is an Accessor?

An accessor method returns the value of a private attribute.

### Pattern

```java
public DataType getAttributeName() {
    return attributeName;
}
```

### Example

```java
public int getMark() {
    return mark;
}
```

### Explanation

| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `public` | outside code can call the method |
| `int` | the method returns an integer |
| `getMark()` | getter method name |
| `return mark;` | sends back the private attribute value |

---

## 7. Accessor Example

### Student.java

```java
public class Student {
    private String name;
    private int mark;

    public Student(String name, int mark) {
        this.name = name;
        this.mark = mark;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getMark() {
        return mark;
    }
}
```

### TestStudent.java

```java
public class TestStudent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student("Alice", 85);

        System.out.println(s1.getName());
        System.out.println(s1.getMark());
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
Alice
85
```

Outside code cannot directly read `name` and `mark`, but it can use getter methods.

---

## 8. Mutator Methods

## 8.1 What is a Mutator?

A mutator method changes the value of a private attribute.

### Pattern

```java
public void setAttributeName(DataType newValue) {
    attributeName = newValue;
}
```

### Example Without Validation

```java
public void setName(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}
```

### Example With Validation

```java
public void setMark(int mark) {
    if (mark >= 0 && mark <= 100) {
        this.mark = mark;
    }
}
```

### Explanation

| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `public` | outside code can call the method |
| `void` | setter usually does not return a value |
| `setMark(int mark)` | receives a new mark |
| `if (...)` | checks whether the mark is valid |
| `this.mark = mark;` | updates the object's private attribute |

---

## 9. Mutator Example with Validation

### Student.java

```java
public class Student {
    private String name;
    private int mark;

    public Student(String name, int mark) {
        this.name = name;
        setMark(mark);
    }

    public int getMark() {
        return mark;
    }

    public void setMark(int mark) {
        if (mark >= 0 && mark <= 100) {
            this.mark = mark;
        }
    }
}
```

### TestStudent.java

```java
public class TestStudent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student("Alice", 85);

        System.out.println(s1.getMark());

        s1.setMark(90);
        System.out.println(s1.getMark());

        s1.setMark(150);
        System.out.println(s1.getMark());
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
85
90
90
```

The invalid mark `150` is rejected, so the mark stays at `90`.

