## 11. Why Use Getters and Setters?

| Reason | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Protect data | Attributes stay private |
| Control changes | Setters can validate values |
| Improve maintainability | Rules are kept inside the class |
| Hide internal details | Outside code does not need to know implementation |
| Support read-only data | Provide getter but no setter |
| Support safe updates | Allow changes only through approved methods |

### Exam-friendly sentence

Accessors and mutators allow controlled access to private attributes while supporting encapsulation.

---

## 12. Getter Naming Convention

Getter methods usually start with `get`.

| Attribute | Getter |
|---|---|
| `name` | `getName()` |
| `mark` | `getMark()` |
| `balance` | `getBalance()` |
| `price` | `getPrice()` |

### Boolean Getter

For Boolean attributes, Java often uses `is`.

```java
private boolean active;

public boolean isActive() {
    return active;
}
```

Both ideas are common:

```java
getActive()
isActive()
```

For beginners, `isActive()` is often clearer for Boolean values.

---

## 13. Setter Naming Convention

Setter methods usually start with `set`.

| Attribute | Setter |
|---|---|
| `name` | `setName(String name)` |
| `mark` | `setMark(int mark)` |
| `balance` | `setBalance(double balance)` |
| `price` | `setPrice(double price)` |

### Important

Not every attribute should always have a setter.

If an attribute should not be changed after object creation, do not provide a setter.

---

## 14. Read-only Attributes

A read-only attribute can be read but not changed from outside the class.

### Example: Student ID

```java
public class Student {
    private String studentId;
    private String name;

    public Student(String studentId, String name) {
        this.studentId = studentId;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getStudentId() {
        return studentId;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

### Why No `setStudentId()`?

A student ID usually should not change after the object is created.

| Attribute | Getter? | Setter? | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| `studentId` | yes | no | ID should be read-only |
| `name` | yes | yes | name may need to change |

---

## 15. Write-only or Restricted Attributes

Sometimes an attribute should be changed but not read directly.

Example: password.

```java
private String password;

public void setPassword(String password) {
    if (password.length() >= 8) {
        this.password = password;
    }
}
```

A program may avoid providing:

```java
getPassword()
```

because returning a password directly may be unsafe.

::: warning Security Note
This is a simple teaching example. Real password handling needs hashing and stronger security practices.
:::

