## 23. Exam-style Questions

### Question 1 [4 marks]

Define accessor and mutator methods.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

An accessor method, also called a getter, returns the value of a private attribute. A mutator method, also called a setter, changes the value of a private attribute, often after validating the new value. These methods allow controlled access to object data.

</details>

---

### Question 2 [5 marks]

Explain how accessors and mutators support encapsulation.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

Accessors and mutators support encapsulation by allowing attributes to remain private while still giving outside code controlled access. A getter can return an attribute value without allowing direct modification. A setter can check or validate a new value before storing it. This protects object state and reduces the risk of invalid data.

</details>

---

### Question 3 [6 marks]

Write a getter and a validated setter for a private attribute:

```java
private int mark;
```

A valid mark is from 0 to 100 inclusive.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

```java
public int getMark() {
    return mark;
}

public void setMark(int mark) {
    if (mark >= 0 && mark <= 100) {
        this.mark = mark;
    }
}
```

Possible marks:

- getter is public
- getter return type is `int`
- getter returns `mark`
- setter is public and void
- setter receives parameter
- setter validates range before assignment
- uses `this.mark = mark` correctly

</details>

---

### Question 4 [6 marks]

Explain why a student ID might have a getter but no setter.

<details>
<summary>Mark Scheme Style Answer</summary>

A student ID is usually assigned when the student record is created and should not normally change. Providing a getter allows the ID to be read when needed. Not providing a setter prevents outside code from changing the ID accidentally or incorrectly. This supports encapsulation and helps protect the consistency of the object state.

</details>

